  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1211 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThanksgiving week will feature below-average temperatures--\nparticularly overnight lows--each day. Global models show\nconsensus in digging additional shortwaves through the central\nCONUS throughout the week, allowing a cold polar airmass to\nstick around. Latest NBM percentile data show only modest\ninterquartile spreads of 3 to 4 degrees, indicating fairly high\nforecast confidence. Daily highs will likely fail to reach 50\ndegrees, while overnight lows will drop into the 20s. In fact, \nNBM QMD data are depicting medium to high probabilities (50-80%)\nof temperatures dropping into the teens across the eastern \nOzarks by mid-week. Indeed, Thanksgiving morning appears it will\nbe quite cold with temperatures in the teens and 20s across the\nentire forecast area. \n\nA glance at ensemble clusters still does not reveal any clear\nsignal in the evolution of the synoptic pattern by late week\nother than a general consensus for change. A small number of\nmembers continue to suggest an open door to precipitation\nchances by Friday, but they are currently in the minority, and\nour PoPs remain less than 20% through the long term period.",
